BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Matriarchal Messages for the Masses
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260928T000000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20270331T235959
UID:matriarchal-messages-masses@ocadu.ca
DESCRIPTION:A community-centred pop-up project initiated by Amy Wong that 
 celebrates the wisdom passed down through mothers\, aunties\, elders\, car
 egivers\, and chosen family.\n\nMMM! Matriarchal Messages for the Masses M
 MM! (Matriarchal Messages for the Masses) is a community-centred pop-up pr
 oject initiated by Amy Wong that celebrates the wisdom passed down through
  mothers\, aunties\, elders\, caregivers\, and chosen family. Through an o
 pen call and poster making workshop\, you are invited to share matriarchal
  messages\, everyday teachings\, reminders\, and words of care. Drawing es
 pecially from QT2S+/BIPOC knowledge traditions\, selected submissions will
  be transformed into a series of collaboratively produced screen-printed T
 -shirts and totes. Part wearable tribute\, part living archive\, MMM! brin
 gs intergenerational knowledge into public space and honours the voices th
 at shape our cultures and communities. Sept 28-29 MMM! (Matriarchal Messag
 es for the Masses) Project Launch in the Lobby at 100 McCaul. November 2-6
 : MMM! Poster Workshop\, Lobby 100 McCaul (led by Amy Wong and Tannis Niel
 sen\, FOA) January 2027 dates TBA: Screen printing workshops (led by Amy W
 ong and Shannon Gerard\, FOA) Feb 2027 dates TBA: Pop-up shop with proceed
 s to be donated to Sistering and the Barbara Schlifer Commemorative Clinic
URL:https://www.ocadu.ca/events-and-exhibitions/matriarchal-messages-masse
 s
X-SOURCE:OCAD University
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Art Creates Change - Bonnie Devine
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260924T190057
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260924T210057
UID:art-creates-change-bonnie-devine@ocadu.ca
DESCRIPTION:OCAD University’s Faculty of Art presents: ART CREATES CHANG
 E The Kim Pruesse Speakers Series\n\nOCAD University’s Faculty of Art pr
 esents: ART CREATES CHANGE The Kim Pruesse Speakers Series Bonnie Devine A
 rtist Talk & Reception: Thursday\, September 24\, 7 pm OCAD University Aud
 itorium 100 McCaul St.\, M5T 1W1\, Level 1\, Room 190 Free to the public W
 heelchair accessible Art Creates Change is a long-running lecture series\,
  now in its 20th year\, sponsored by the Faculty of Art at OCAD University
 . The series focuses on artists whose work engages and challenges the conv
 entions of creative practice and political imperatives\, and their various
  locations of agency. Events are often co-sponsored with programs and orga
 nizations both within and beyond the university. This year\, in recognitio
 n of OCAD University’s 150th anniversary\, we are focusing on three facu
 lty members: Bonnie Devine\, Richard Fung and Lillian Allen\, three artist
 s whose work has helped shape not only OCAD\, but also the larger culture 
 and political conversations of our time. Their creative\, academic and pol
 itical work organically engages urgent public conversations about represen
 tation\, justice\, equity and relational power\, and embodies OCAD’s vis
 ion of “a vibrant community of bold\, curious and compassionate artists\
 , designers and scholars imagining a creative\, joyful\, equitable and sus
 tainable world.” Events are often co-sponsored with programs and organiz
 ations both within and beyond the university. Each event includes an artis
 t presentation\, Q&A\, and a following reception. The events are free and 
 open to the public and university communities. About Bonnie Devine Bonnie 
 Devine’s work examines colonial legacies\, offers counter narratives\, a
 nd proposes an animate mapping of the Great Lakes and Woodland region of N
 orth America. Her cross-disciplinary practice engages various media\, from
  painting to sculpture to video to writing\, giving voice to the lands\, w
 aters\, and histories on which she works and from which her art emerges. H
 er work has been recognized with numerous awards\, including a Governor Ge
 neral’s Award in Visual and Media Arts and an Ontario Lieutenant Governo
 r’s Heritage Award. Her art has been exhibited nationally and internatio
 nally\, is in a number of institutional collections\, and this year is inc
 luded in the 2026 Biennales in both Venice and Toronto. Bonnie is a member
  of the Genaabaajing Serpent River Anishinaabe First Nation\, and is the F
 ounding Chair of OCAD University’s Indigenous Visual Culture program. Th
 e Gift - Lake Ontario\, 2022 Battle for the Woodlands 2015 Land in War 202
 4 Battle of the Little Big Horn\, Montana Territory\, 1876 Treaty Robe for
  Tecumseh\, 2013\n\nThe Art Creates Change 2026 is supported by Onsite Gal
 lery OCAD U Art & Social Change INTM: LIFE STUDIES SPECIALIZATION in the F
 aculty of Art through a generous donation of the Joan and Clifford Hatch F
 oundation. Follow OCAD U Faculty of Art on Social Media 2026 Co-programmer
 s: b.h. Yael & Min Sook Lee
LOCATION:Main Building & Rosalie Sharp Centre for Design (MCA) 100 McCaul 
 Street Auditorium\, MCA190
URL:https://www.ocadu.ca/events-and-exhibitions/art-creates-change-bonnie-
 devine
X-SOURCE:OCAD University
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Women of Wampum
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260923T123000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260923T140000
UID:women-wampum@ocadu.ca
DESCRIPTION:In conjunction with the Gwangju Biennale Canada Pavilion exhib
 ition Skennen'kó:wa ken? Do You Carry Great Peace? Onsite Gallery present
 s Women of Wampum with artist Katsitsakatste Lindsay Delaronde.\n\nIn conj
 unction with the Gwangju Biennale Canada Pavilion exhibition Skennen'kó:w
 a ken? Do You Carry Great Peace? Onsite Gallery presents Women of Wampum\,
  a lecture and workshop led by artist Katsitsakatste Lindsay Delaronde exp
 loring the enduring role of Haudenosaunee women as spiritual leaders\, kno
 wledge keepers\, and architects of peace. Grounded in the teachings of Sky
  World\, the Creation Story\, the Great Law of Peace\, and the Peacemaker 
 narrative\, Delaronde examines how Haudenosaunee knowledge continues to sh
 ape governance through balance\, reciprocity\, relational accountability\,
  and collective responsibility. Visit Registration Page About Lindsay Dela
 ronde Katsitsakatste Lindsay Delaronde is Haudenosaunee\, a member of the 
 wolf clan\, and born to Kahnawake community. Kahnawake\, which translates 
 from the Kanienkehaka language into “people by the rapids\,” exists by
  the St. Lawrence River and extends its territory outwards to the five oth
 er neighboring nations. Kanienkehaka\, also known as the Mohawk nation\, a
 re an integral part of the Haudenosaunee confederacy alongside the Oneida\
 , Onondaga\, Cayuga\, Seneca\, and the last to join the confederacy\, the 
 Tuscarora. Being part of the confederacy representing the Haudenosaunee pe
 ople\, also known as “People who build” (Lyons\, Mohawk\, Barreiro\, 2
 005) and bound by the cosmologies of The Creation Story\, the Great Law of
  Peace\, the concept of the Seventh Generation\, and Treaty Making\, which
  shaped her understanding of governance\, relationality\, and place and pu
 rpose in the universe. As a descendant of those who once built palisades\,
  raised longhouses\, and pounded corn\, she is not separate from these cos
 mologies and mythologies—she is their living embodied constellation\, ca
 rrying forward the matrilineal knowledge\, cultural resilience\, and peace
 building that have sustained her people for generations. Delaronde is curr
 ently a PhD candidate within the Indigenous Governance program. As a multi
 -disciplinary artist who values creativity and the ungovernable space of t
 he imagination\, she engages and participates in the field of Indigenous g
 overnance theory and research through her PhD studies with the consciousne
 ss of the Haudenosaunee confederacy woven into the essence of her being. D
 elaronde holds a B.F.A. from the Emily Carr\, Institute of Art & Design\, 
 an M.F.A from the University of Victoria.\, and a Master’s in Indigenous
  Communities Counseling Psychology from the UVIC. She currently holds the 
 position as the Audain Professorship in the Visual Arts department at UVIC
 . Her artistic practice focuses on Indigenous dance and theatre\, land-bas
 ed dramaturgy\, embodiment\, site-specific performance art\, co-creative c
 ollaborative practice\, cultural resurgence and social/political activism 
 and decolonial methodologies in art and research. Her artistic philosophy 
 is rooted within Indigenous aesthetics influenced and shaped by Haudenosau
 nee epistemologies. Her past and present solo and community projects intim
 ately converse with the land\, body\, cosmos\, identity\, eroticism\, sove
 reignty and Indigenous feminisms to forge artistic pathways towards a libe
 ratory existence for self-actualization and self-determination. She weaves
  culture\, art\, history\, ceremony\, healing\, spirituality\, breath\, so
 ng\, movement\, human development and mindfulness into the tapestry of the
  creative process to support the re-visioning of Indigenous futurities of 
 hope and beauty. Transcending the intergenerational soul wound through the
  impasse of the radical creative spirit is what she strives to offer in he
 r work. Her commitment to carrying the message of peace\, power and righte
 ousness is guided by the power of imagination\, creativity\, and creating 
 processes of relational accountability. She hopes to continue contributing
  to peacebuilding and conflict resolution by amplifying community stories 
 through embodied research methodologies\, and honoring kinship through art
 istic expression. About Skennen'kó:wa ken? (Do You Carry Great Peace?) OC
 AD University is representing Canada at the 2026 Gwangju Biennale (Korea)\
 , one of the world’s leading contemporary art exhibitions and Asia’s m
 ost significant biennale. The Kanien’kehá:ka-led exhibition\, Skennen'k
 ó:wa ken? (Do You Carry Great Peace?) features works by acclaimed Kanien
 ’kehá:ka artists and alums Shelley Niro\, Melissa General and Hannah Cl
 aus and is curated by Ryan Rice\, Onsite Gallery. The exhibition runs from
  September 5 to November 15\, 2026. The pavilion is generously sponsored b
 y philanthropist David Binet. Onsite Gallery is generously supported by Th
 e Delaney Family. We also acknowledge the support of the Ontario Arts Coun
 cil\, an agency of the Ontario Government and City of Toronto through Toro
 nto Arts Council.
LOCATION:Onsite Gallery (RHD) 199 Richmond Street West 199 Richmond Street
  West
URL:https://www.ocadu.ca/events-and-exhibitions/women-wampum
X-SOURCE:OCAD University
END:VEVENT
